                <pb facs="0255" n="181" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v01/pg181"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72119/783</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-08-30T12:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 30, 1917, noon</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Received 4.10 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>7060. The reception of the President’s answer to the Pope by the Government, the
                    press, and, so far as I can yet tell, by the whole British public, is more
                    enthusiastic than the reception of any previous declaration with the exception
                    only of the President’s speech asking Congress to declare war. It meets with
                    universal enthusiastic approval.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
